A||N||3||mblackwell||Looking for a cheap reliable car.||||1||
Z||000000||mblackwell||10-11-2001||01:22 PM||||My mom's transmission quit working and she needs another car, but she can only spend about $1000-1500.<P>All it has to do is take her 5 miles to work and back every day, and shopping ect.  It doesn't need to have looks or power, just reliability and 4 doors.<P>What make/model used cars do you reccomend?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||10-11-2001||02:16 PM||||I'd look at Toyotas, maybe a Corolla. I've had a great deal of luck with my p/u.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||10-11-2001||03:06 PM||||Corrolla's and Civic's always get good reliability ratings.  I also know some people who have had Cavaliers that have lasted forever without suffering problems.  However, at your price level, I wouldn't really rule out anything and just look for something that "looks" likeits in good shape.  <P>What I mean by looks good is first off the interior.  First off is milage.  I don't care how it was driven, if its got 150K on the clock its going to be a lot closer to the grave than a car with 50K on it.  Its level of cleanliness can be easily altered, but look for wear on the floormats, seats and carpets and pedals.  The greater the wear, the greater the car was used.  Check for overspray underneath/in wheel wells for signs of major accidents.  Also really listen to the engine and take it for a good drive... does ity idle/drive smooth or rough.  Does the transmission feel like its shifting ok or not.  <P>Basically, use your gut feeling on the car.  If something looks/feels/sounds suspicious, keep looking around.  With a little patience its amazing how good of a car you can come up with for very little $$.  Good luck!!||||reg||1
